- in_text_citation: [(Cruces-Solís et al., 2018-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a149aa6bb67d031b494d792fe9e4b448abf995f7)
- venue: PLoS Biology
- citation count: 24
[method]
The paper used a sound-exposure and auditory discrimination method in which subjects were exposed to predictable or random sounds in an audiobox to generate sound-context associations, then tested for spontaneous frequency discrimination acuity and recorded with single-unit electrophysiology during acoustic stimulation. Neural responses were analyzed with ROC analysis and classification-accuracy modeling to quantify frequency coding and discrimination, and simultaneous cortical inactivation with inferior colliculus recordings was used to assess the contribution of cortical input. This relates to the proposed plan because it combines auditory discrimination behavior, electrophysiological recordings, and causal cortical manipulation, and it uses ROC-based discriminability metrics similar to the proposed single-neuron ROC analysis, although the paper focuses on sound-exposure-induced inferior colliculus plasticity rather than adolescent versus adult learning or optogenetic silencing.
[results]
The supplied section headings do not provide numerical values, sample sizes, or statistical tests. Reported directional outcomes were: predictable sound exposure generated sound-context associations; sound exposure increased evoked responses in the inferior colliculus; sound exposure led to a global suprathreshold shift in best frequency; predictable and random sound exposure increased response gain through different mechanisms; increased response gain affected population activity and structural tuning; differential response-gain increases produced differential frequency coding and discrimination; predictable sound exposure decreased behavioral spontaneous frequency discrimination acuity; predictable sound exposure produced long-lasting changes in postsynaptic excitation/inhibition balance; corticofugal input had a minor role in collicular plasticity induced by predictable sound exposure; and predictable exposure did not lead to changes in the cochlear nucleus or auditory cortex.
[context]
Experimental conditions included an audiobox sound-exposure setup, predictable versus random sound exposure, an LI paradigm, electrophysiology in the inferior colliculus with additional single-unit recordings from the cochlear nucleus and auditory cortex, acoustic stimulation during recordings, and analyses including ROC, classification accuracy, PPI, and gene expression. The supplied metadata does not specify species, age, sex, sample size, exact sound frequencies, exposure durations, electrode types, or statistical thresholds, so those details cannot be reported.
[end related work 1]

