- citation count: 4
[method]
The paper used an auditory discrimination behavioral paradigm in mice combined with head-fixed electrophysiological recording from auditory cortex. Mice were trained to discriminate auditory stimuli, including pure tones and natural sounds, in a behavioral task, and A1 population activity was recorded during task performance. The method included a behavioral setup and surgical procedure for head-fixed recording, auditory stimulus presentation, behavioral data analysis, electrophysiological analysis, and population coding analyses using an Independent Basis Functions generative model, Fisher information, and linear readout. The paper also discusses the Educage home-cage system as a way to simplify auditory training, which is directly relevant to the proposed use of Educage for initial behavioral characterization before head-fixed simultaneous neural recordings.
[results]
The supplied paper excerpt does not provide numerical effect sizes, sample sizes, or p-values. It reports that mice learned to discriminate pure tones, that A1 population responses to pure tones could be described by an IBF generative model, and that perceptual learning of natural sounds was associated with sparser A1 population responses and learning-induced plasticity in parvalbumin-positive neurons.
[context]
The experimental context was mouse auditory cortex, with focus on A1 and auditory stimuli consisting of pure tones and natural sounds. The relevant conditions involved head-fixed task performance and electrophysiological recording, with analysis of single-unit and population responses. Exact probe type, animal age, sample size, and stimulus parameters are not specified in the supplied excerpt. This context supports the proposed research plan's design of training adolescent and adult mice on a Go/No-Go pure-tone discrimination task and recording from auditory cortical layers 5 and 6 with high-density Neuropixels probes to separate age and learning effects.
[end related work 1]

