- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 9
[method]
The paper used a tactile discrimination setup and learning paradigm in which freely moving mice learned to discriminate apertures with their whiskers and to learn different task rules. The behavioral paradigm was a sensory discrimination task in which correct and incorrect responses could be mapped onto hit and false-alarm categories, supporting signal-detection-theory quantification of discrimination performance and response bias. This is directly analogous to the proposed auditory Go/No-Go pure-tone discrimination plan, where d-prime, hit rate, false-alarm rate, response bias, and behavioral variability will be used to separate sensory discrimination from response tendency.
[results]
Mice learned to discriminate the apertures and could learn different task rules, indicating that the tactile discrimination paradigm produced reliable behavioral performance suitable for signal-detection-theory analysis. The provided excerpt does not report exact numerical hit-rate, false-alarm-rate, d-prime, bias, or statistical significance values, so no specific quantitative outcome can be extracted from the supplied text.
[context]
The task was performed by freely moving mice in a tactile discrimination apparatus with apertures, with training over multiple days and rule learning. The study combined behavioral training with electrophysiological and optical recordings, and included sensory-deprivation controls such as whisker plucking and lidocaine numbing of the whisker pad, as well as barrel-cortex and olfactory-bulb lesions to establish that performance depended on whisker-mediated tactile processing. These conditions make the paper relevant to the proposed adolescent/adult auditory discrimination study by demonstrating a comparable behavioral quantification framework in a sensory discrimination task, although the stimulus modality and freely moving tactile setup differ from the head-fixed auditory recordings planned.
[end related work 1]

