- venue: PLoS Biology
- citation count: 24
[method]
The paper used single-unit electrophysiological recordings in the inferior colliculus of mice during predictable and random sound exposure, with acoustic stimulation and subsequent analysis of evoked responses, best-frequency tuning, response gain, population activity, and frequency discrimination. Discrimination was quantified using ROC analysis, and postsynaptic excitation/inhibition balance was assessed using gene expression analysis. The paper also used simultaneous cortical inactivation together with inferior colliculus recordings to test the contribution of corticofugal input to collicular plasticity. This is related to the proposed method because both use auditory neural recordings and ROC-based discrimination metrics, and to the research plan because both compare neural representations during auditory discrimination; however, the paper recorded subcortical inferior colliculus rather than high-density Neuropixels recordings from auditory cortical layers 5 and 6, and it did not compare adolescent and adult mice performing a Go/No-Go discrimination task.
[results]
The available section headings report that sound exposure increased evoked responses in the inferior colliculus; predictable and random sound exposure increased response gain through different mechanisms; the increase in response gain affected population activity and structural tuning; differential increases in response gain produced differential frequency coding and discrimination; predictable sound exposure decreased behavioral spontaneous frequency discrimination acuity; predictable sound exposure produced long-lasting changes in postsynaptic excitation/inhibition balance; and cortical inactivation indicated that corticofugal input had a minor role on collicular plasticity induced by predictable sound exposure. No quantitative values, effect sizes, sample sizes, or statistical significance tests are present in the supplied excerpt.
[context]
The experimental context involved mice subjected to predictable versus random sound exposure, electrophysiological recordings in the inferior colliculus, acoustic stimulation, ROC analysis, and gene expression analysis. The paper also included simultaneous cortical inactivation and inferior colliculus recordings to test corticofugal input, as well as cochlear nucleus and anterior colliculus recordings, but the supplied excerpt does not specify probe type, electrode configuration, animal age, task difficulty, or cortical recording sites. Therefore, the paper provides a related but not directly equivalent method to the proposed adolescent/adult auditory-cortex Neuropixels discrimination recordings.
[end related work 1]

