[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Cruces-Solís et al., 2018-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a149aa6bb67d031b494d792fe9e4b448abf995f7)
- venue: PLoS Biology
- citation count: 24
[method]
The paper used a sound-exposure paradigm in mice to induce auditory plasticity and then evaluated the consequences with in vivo electrophysiology, behavioral frequency discrimination, and molecular analysis. Mice were exposed to predictable or random sounds, and evoked responses were recorded from the inferior colliculus. Frequency tuning, response gain, population activity, and structural tuning were characterized, and frequency coding and discrimination were assessed using ROC analysis. Behavioral spontaneous frequency discrimination acuity was also measured, and gene expression analysis was used to examine long-lasting changes in postsynaptic excitation/inhibition balance. This method is related to the proposed research method because it uses exposure-induced changes and ROC-based discrimination metrics to separate sensory plasticity from baseline auditory processing, analogous to comparing naive and expert mice to isolate learning-related effects, although the paper does not use age groups or a trained Go/No-Go expert condition.
[results]
Sound exposure increased evoked responses in the inferior colliculus. Predictable and random sound exposure both increased response gain, but through different mechanisms. The increase in response gain altered population activity and was reflected in structural tuning. Differential increases in response gain produced differential frequency coding and discrimination. Predictable sound exposure decreased behavioral spontaneous frequency discrimination acuity. Predictable sound exposure also produced long-lasting changes in postsynaptic excitation/inhibition balance. No quantitative effect sizes, sample sizes, or statistical significance values are available in the provided excerpt.
[context]
The experimental system was mouse auditory midbrain, specifically the inferior colliculus, with predictable versus random sound exposure conditions. The study combined electrophysiological recordings, behavioral frequency discrimination, ROC analysis, and gene expression analysis. Additional experiments indicated that corticofugal input had a minor role in the collicular plasticity and that predictable exposure did not lead to changes in the cochlear nucleus or anterior colliculus, suggesting collicular-specific effects. The provided excerpt does not specify probe types, exact stimulus parameters, animal ages, sample sizes, or statistical thresholds, and it does not include the naive-versus-expert age-group design proposed in the research plan.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: To compare auditory learning and cortical processing between adolescent and adult mice, we will employ a comprehensive approach combining behavioral training, electrophysiological recordings, and optogenetic manipulations.

