- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 13
[method]
The paper used a behavioral target-in-background auditory detection task combined with behavioral electrophysiological recordings to compare behavioral and neuronal detection performance. Single-unit discriminability was assessed using ROC analysis of stimulus-related activity, separating target-present from target-absent trials, and population-level trial-outcome decoding was assessed using population response metrics and a population classifier. Additional linear-nonlinear modeling was used to relate population activity to behavior. This is directly analogous to the proposed use of ROC analysis for single-neuron discriminability and linear discriminant analysis for population-level decoding in an adolescent/adult auditory Go/No-Go discrimination task.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not include numerical ROC AUC values, classifier accuracy, d-prime values, or statistical significance. It reports qualitatively that behavioral detection was modulated by background contrast, that auditory cortex was necessary for detection in background noise, that cortical codes predicted individual behavioral performance, and that cortical gain predicted behavioral performance.
[context]
The experimental context was mice performing a target-in-background auditory detection task with varying background contrast, with simultaneous auditory cortical recordings during behavioral trials. Analyses included behavioral detection metrics, single-unit ROC analysis, population response metrics, a population classifier, and linear-nonlinear modeling; specific probe type, animal numbers, and statistical thresholds are not provided in the supplied excerpt.
[end related work 1]

