[method]
The paper used a whisker-based object-detection behavioral paradigm in head-fixed mice combined with longitudinal 2-photon calcium imaging to compare neural representations under conditioning versus repeated stimulus exposure. The imaging setup involved virus injection and cranial window implantation, intrinsic signal imaging, 2-photon calcium imaging, and histological confirmation of the imaged barrel cortex location. Neural activity was analyzed using data processing and analysis procedures, transient detection, identification of temporal tuning, and whisking analysis. This method is related to the proposed research method because both use a discrimination task and analyze neural discriminability or decodability of stimulus and choice signals. However, the paper’s approach is not identical to the proposed method: it uses optical calcium imaging in barrel cortex during whisker-based object detection rather than Neuropixels electrophysiology in auditory cortical layers 5 and 6 during auditory Go/No-Go tone discrimination, and the supplied text does not explicitly state that ROC analysis or linear discriminant analysis were used.
[results]
The supplied section titles report that conditioning, but not repeated stimulus exposure, enhanced object representation; learning switched the response category of longitudinally tracked neurons; training enhanced decodability of stimulus and choice; population activity encoded time; movement did not account for learning-dependent changes in activity; and temporal surprise was an effective driver of neuronal activity. No numerical ROC areas, LDA decoding accuracies, d-prime values, hit rates, false alarm rates, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied excerpt, so quantitative comparison to the proposed ROC and LDA discriminability analyses cannot be made.
