[method]
The paper used optogenetic inactivation of mouse auditory cortex during a signal-in-noise detection task. Mice received bilateral AAV-mediated expression of an inhibitory opsin in auditory cortex and bilateral optical fiber implants. During behavioral testing, light was delivered on a randomized subset of trials to silence auditory cortex, and performance was compared between light and no-light trials using signal detection theory metrics such as d-prime, hit rate, and false alarm rate. A related experiment inactivated cortical inhibitory neurons to test their specific contribution to detection.
[results]
Inactivation of auditory cortex impaired detection of acoustic signals in noise, reducing detection performance relative to no-light trials. Inactivation of cortical inhibitory neurons also impaired detection, supporting a causal contribution of cortical inhibition to auditory signal detection. The supplied paper excerpt does not include exact numerical d-prime values, effect sizes, or p-values, so quantitative magnitudes cannot be reported.
[context]
The experimental context was trained mice performing an acoustic signal-in-noise detection task, with bilateral auditory cortex implants and randomized light delivery to avoid expectation or trial-order effects. This method directly parallels the proposed research plan's optogenetic silencing of auditory cortex using AAV inhibitory opsins, bilateral optical fiber implants, and light on 50% of randomized trials, and provides a precedent for testing the causal necessity of auditory cortex in auditory discrimination tasks across adolescent and adult mice.
[end related work 2]

Processing method: To establish causal necessity of auditory cortex, we will use optogenetic silencing of auditory cortex, employing AAV-mediated expression of inhibitory opsins with bilateral optical fiber implants, and test task performance by applying light stimulation in 50% of trials in a randomized fashion.
Processing method: To separate age-related effects from learning-related effects, we will record from both naive and expert mice in each age group and compare neural responses between naive mice (recorded after tone association but before discrimination learning) and expert mice (recorded after completing the full training protocol).
