[method]
The paper used a two-alternative forced-choice behavioral task in awake, head-fixed mice and applied behavioral analysis and statistical modeling to decompose choice behavior into task-dependent, stimulus-related, and experimental-variable contributions. Optogenetic experiments used bilateral blue-light activation of PV interneurons to inactivate auditory cortex during the task. This is related to the proposed method because the research plan also uses optogenetic silencing of auditory cortex and requires control virus injections, counterbalanced sex and housing, and hierarchical statistical modeling of repeated behavioral measurements.
[results]
The behavioral modeling indicated that task-dependent, stimulus-related, and experimental variables contributed to mouse choice behavior. Optogenetic inactivation of auditory cortex reduced perceived loudness, producing perceptual hyposensitivity. Noise-induced cochlear sensorineural damage was associated with loudness hyperacusis, and the hyperacusis was stable across time without a decrease in reaction time. The provided excerpt does not include specific effect sizes, p-values, or sample sizes.
[context]
Experiments were conducted in awake, head-fixed mice after survival surgeries, using a two-alternative forced-choice task and blue-light optogenetic activation of PV interneurons for bilateral auditory cortex inactivation. The relevant sections focus on repeated choice measurements and experimental variables, but the excerpt does not specify the exact mixed-effects model, random effects for co-housed animals, or counterbalancing details.
